1
00:00:01.001 --> 00:00:06.001
Documents do not always present information in a strictly top-to-bottom, left-to-right order.

2
00:00:06.973 --> 00:00:09.776
In this lesson, you will learn how to

3
00:00:09.776 --> 00:00:12.579
use models like LayoutReader to detect text ordering.

4
00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:17.884
After identifying a document's layout and locating and ordering the text,

5
00:00:18.184 --> 00:00:20.353
you will use a VLM to

6
00:00:20.353 --> 00:00:22.889
develop a holistic understanding of the document.

7
00:00:23.456 --> 00:00:25.558
Okay, let's get to it.

8
00:00:25.658 --> 00:00:28.762
In lesson two, Andrea walked us through the evolution of OCR,

9
00:00:28.862 --> 00:00:32.866
from traditional engines like Tesseract to modern deep learning systems.

10
00:00:32.966 --> 00:00:35.068
Now that we understand how text is extracted,

11
00:00:35.168 --> 00:00:37.170
we're ready for the next challenge:

12
00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:39.372
documents with complex layouts.

13
00:00:39.472 --> 00:00:41.775
We'll start by grounding ourselves in what

14
00:00:41.775 --> 00:00:44.077
layout detection and reading order actually mean,

15
00:00:44.177 --> 00:00:47.047
and why they matter so much for document intelligence.

16
00:00:47.147 --> 00:00:50.750
Then, we'll look at how modern systems try to solve these problems

17
00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:54.354
using learning-based approaches instead of simple heuristics.

18
00:00:55.021 --> 00:00:57.524
cover real-world challenges, dealing with forms,

19
00:00:57.524 --> 00:00:59.826
tables, figures, handwriting,

20
00:00:59.826 --> 00:01:03.830
and multilingual documents and how they can be solved with specialized models.

21
00:01:03.830 --> 00:01:07.934
From there, we'll introduce Vision-Language Models and talk about how they differ

22
00:01:07.934 --> 00:01:10.537
from traditional language-only models.

23
00:01:10.537 --> 00:01:14.040
Finally, we'll discuss how you can combine these techniques

24
00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:17.243
into a hybrid architecture that you'll implement in the lab.

25
00:01:17.243 --> 00:01:19.813
Let's begin with the pipeline that many teams

26
00:01:19.813 --> 00:01:21.915
and organizations still rely on today.

27
00:01:21.915 --> 00:01:24.517
First, you extract the text from the document.

28
00:01:25.085 --> 00:01:26.920
Then you pass that text into

29
00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:29.089
a language model and ask it questions.

30
00:01:29.456 --> 00:01:32.859
On the surface, this feels reasonable, simple, even elegant.

31
00:01:33.293 --> 00:01:36.696
But the problem is that most text extraction can be destructive.

32
00:01:37.297 --> 00:01:39.999
The moment we flatten a document, structure is lost.

33
00:01:40.066 --> 00:01:42.368
columns and rows get mixed together,

34
00:01:42.368 --> 00:01:45.071
tables become meaningless floating text blobs.

35
00:01:45.071 --> 00:01:47.173
Captions detached from figures,

36
00:01:47.173 --> 00:01:49.676
and reading order becomes unpredictable.

37
00:01:50.076 --> 00:01:52.879
For complex documents, financial reports,

38
00:01:52.879 --> 00:01:54.981
research papers, and legal contracts.

39
00:01:55.281 --> 00:02:00.281
OCR plus an LLM simply doesn't have enough context to reason correctly.

40
00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:03.456
Layout detection or Document Layout Analysis is

41
00:02:03.456 --> 00:02:06.126
the first step toward fixing that problem.

42
00:02:06.526 --> 00:02:09.796
Rather than treating a document as a corpus of raw text,

43
00:02:10.130 --> 00:02:13.800
layout detection identifies meaningful regions on the page

44
00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:17.103
and figures out where they are and what they represent.

45
00:02:17.403 --> 00:02:22.308
It distinguishes between paragraphs, tables, figures, headers, footers, and

46
00:02:22.342 --> 00:02:26.346
captions. In other words, we're no longer just extracting content,

47
00:02:26.346 --> 00:02:28.848
we're understanding the structure of the document.

48
00:02:28.848 --> 00:02:31.151
This turns out to be incredibly important.

49
00:02:31.151 --> 00:02:34.154
Preserving layout prevents different sections of text

50
00:02:34.154 --> 00:02:36.356
from getting mixed together and convoluted.

51
00:02:36.356 --> 00:02:39.859
It preserves the narrative flow in multi-column documents.

52
00:02:39.859 --> 00:02:42.862
It allows you to target specific parts of a page

53
00:02:42.862 --> 00:02:46.366
like totals in a table or key fields in a form.

54
00:02:46.366 --> 00:02:49.169
The core idea here is simple but powerful.

55
00:02:49.702 --> 00:02:52.505
layout is important. Once you throw it away,

56
00:02:52.505 --> 00:02:55.508
understanding becomes fragile and error prone.

57
00:02:56.109 --> 00:02:58.411
If you look at real world documents, such

58
00:02:58.411 --> 00:03:01.014
as the ones you've seen in the previous lab,

59
00:03:01.014 --> 00:03:03.016
they're rarely just blocks of text.

60
00:03:03.016 --> 00:03:06.419
They include columns, tables, charts, images,

61
00:03:06.419 --> 00:03:10.523
captions, headers, footers, stamps, and annotations.

62
00:03:11.024 --> 00:03:13.793
When you explicitly detect and label these components,

63
00:03:13.793 --> 00:03:17.197
downstream models know what kind of information they're looking at

64
00:03:17.197 --> 00:03:19.399
before they try to reason over them.

65
00:03:19.599 --> 00:03:22.202
Once we know what's on the page and where it is,

66
00:03:22.402 --> 00:03:24.904
the next question is how should it be read.

67
00:03:25.305 --> 00:03:27.207
Layout tells us where things are.

68
00:03:27.473 --> 00:03:29.509
Reading order tells us in what

69
00:03:29.509 --> 00:03:31.878
sequence a human would actually read them.

70
00:03:32.378 --> 00:03:36.449
This matters enormously in multi-column layouts or documents

71
00:03:36.449 --> 00:03:38.451
with floating figures and captions.

72
00:03:38.952 --> 00:03:40.954
Without a reliable reading order,

73
00:03:40.954 --> 00:03:44.257
even a clean layout still leaves room for ambiguity.

74
00:03:44.557 --> 00:03:47.126
Historically, reading order was handled with rules.

75
00:03:47.627 --> 00:03:50.330
You would sort regions top to bottom, left to right,

76
00:03:50.630 --> 00:03:54.033
maybe apply an X-Y cut algorithm and hope for the best.

77
00:03:54.234 --> 00:03:56.135
This works for very clean documents,

78
00:03:56.336 --> 00:03:59.739
but breaks down immediately for anything real and complex.

79
00:04:00.139 --> 00:04:02.942
Any columns, sidebars, or floating elements,

80
00:04:03.343 --> 00:04:05.945
and those heuristics start producing nonsense.

81
00:04:06.246 --> 00:04:08.047
That changed with LayoutReader.

82
00:04:08.147 --> 00:04:11.951
Instead of rules, it's a learned model trained on ReadingBank dataset

83
00:04:12.051 --> 00:04:16.055
over 500,000 annotated pages with correct reading sequences.

84
00:04:16.356 --> 00:04:19.359
Each word gets represented as a tuple

85
00:04:19.459 --> 00:04:22.061
containing the word itself and its apparent index.

86
00:04:22.262 --> 00:04:24.631
Along with layout features, word color,

87
00:04:24.731 --> 00:04:26.933
bounding box coordinates, width, and height.

88
00:04:27.033 --> 00:04:28.635
The model learns to predict

89
00:04:28.735 --> 00:04:32.405
the correct reading order from these visual and spatial features.

90
00:04:32.705 --> 00:04:34.907
This handles complex layouts, multi-column structures,

91
00:04:35.208 --> 00:04:38.211
and irregular reading flows

92
00:04:38.311 --> 00:04:40.413
that rules-based system can't manage.

93
00:04:40.713 --> 00:04:42.915
Let's look at how LayoutReader actually works.

94
00:04:43.416 --> 00:04:46.519
The architecture is a sequence-to-sequence model

95
00:04:46.619 --> 00:04:48.921
using LayoutLM as its encoder.

96
00:04:49.289 --> 00:04:52.792
LayoutLM was developed by Microsoft in 2020

97
00:04:53.293 --> 00:04:56.496
and combines text, layout, and visual information.

98
00:04:56.929 --> 00:05:00.433
The purpose is to predict the correct reading order of words

99
00:05:00.433 --> 00:05:02.335
or text lines in a document.

100
00:05:02.635 --> 00:05:05.438
The process takes OCR-produced bounding boxes,

101
00:05:05.438 --> 00:05:07.340
for example from PaddleOCR,

102
00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:10.743
and rearranges the document's token sequence

103
00:05:10.743 --> 00:05:13.146
to reconstruct a human-readable reading order.

104
00:05:13.246 --> 00:05:14.947
The model was trained on ReadingBank,

105
00:05:14.947 --> 00:05:19.452
a benchmark data set Microsoft created specifically for this task.

106
00:05:19.919 --> 00:05:22.922
It contains 500,000 document images

107
00:05:22.922 --> 00:05:25.725
annotated for reading order. You can see the diversity

108
00:05:25.725 --> 00:05:28.928
Research papers with multi-column layouts,

109
00:05:29.329 --> 00:05:31.497
scientific documents with mixed structures,

110
00:05:31.898 --> 00:05:34.500
and financial documents with tables and lists.

111
00:05:34.901 --> 00:05:38.504
It guessed the correct reading sequence despite their varying layouts.

112
00:05:38.905 --> 00:05:42.208
Now, let's talk about why OCR and reading order is not enough.

113
00:05:42.508 --> 00:05:47.213
Reading order detection depends entirely on the input quality from the OCR.

114
00:05:47.714 --> 00:05:49.515
But here is the fundamental limitation.

115
00:05:49.916 --> 00:05:51.517
OCR only captures text.

116
00:05:51.918 --> 00:05:54.220
It misses visual information, images,

117
00:05:54.487 --> 00:05:58.291
charts, diagrams, spatial relationships, and visual context.

118
00:05:58.691 --> 00:06:02.395
So even with perfect reading order, you're working with incomplete information.

119
00:06:02.995 --> 00:06:04.831
So, what does this look like

120
00:06:04.831 --> 00:06:06.999
in practice? You'll see these challenges repeatedly.

121
00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:10.503
Misaligned forms, complex tables, handwriting,

122
00:06:10.903 --> 00:06:15.475
multilingual documents, and figures that require interpretation rather than transcription.

123
00:06:15.775 --> 00:06:19.278
These are exactly the points where OCR-based pipelines fall apart.

124
00:06:19.579 --> 00:06:22.882
And you've already encountered some of these issues directly in the lab.

125
00:06:23.282 --> 00:06:26.753
Forms are a perfect example of why this is hard.

126
00:06:27.053 --> 00:06:30.056
The core challenge is associating labels with values,

127
00:06:30.256 --> 00:06:32.258
especially when they're not adjacent.

128
00:06:32.458 --> 00:06:34.460
You have a few possible approaches.

129
00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:37.263
Template-based extraction using fixed coordinates,

130
00:06:37.363 --> 00:06:40.066
which is fast, but inflexible when layouts change.

131
00:06:40.166 --> 00:06:42.535
Flexible key-value pair detection, using rules

132
00:06:42.535 --> 00:06:45.271
or ML based on proximity and content,

133
00:06:45.371 --> 00:06:47.473
or fine-tune transformers like

134
00:06:47.507 --> 00:06:50.309
LayoutLM, trained on datasets like FUNSD

135
00:06:50.309 --> 00:06:52.412
that learn these relationships automatically.

136
00:06:52.712 --> 00:06:54.414
And you still need computer vision

137
00:06:54.714 --> 00:06:57.417
for non-text elements like checkbox states.

138
00:06:58.184 --> 00:07:00.686
Solving this requires combining layout understanding

139
00:07:00.686 --> 00:07:03.189
with semantic reasoning and visual detection.

140
00:07:03.589 --> 00:07:05.892
Tables are another notorious failure point.

141
00:07:06.392 --> 00:07:08.494
Traditional OCR destroys table integrity

142
00:07:08.494 --> 00:07:11.297
by flattening rows and column relationships.

143
00:07:11.731 --> 00:07:14.233
Once structure is lost, numbers stop making sense.

144
00:07:14.534 --> 00:07:16.235
Modern models tackle this differently.

145
00:07:16.335 --> 00:07:19.639
Table Transformer uses object detection to find tables,

146
00:07:19.939 --> 00:07:22.942
rows, and columns as distinct objects.

147
00:07:23.242 --> 00:07:26.145
TableFormer takes an image-to-sequence approach,

148
00:07:26.245 --> 00:07:29.248
translating table images directly to HTML.

149
00:07:29.549 --> 00:07:33.252
TABLET uses split-and-merge for large, dense tables.

150
00:07:33.352 --> 00:07:34.921
The output can be CSV,

151
00:07:34.987 --> 00:07:37.790
JSON, HTML, or Pandas DataFrame.

152
00:07:37.890 --> 00:07:40.793
so we can reason over data instead of text blobs.

153
00:07:40.993 --> 00:07:44.964
Handwriting and multilingual documents represent two more failure modes.

154
00:07:45.164 --> 00:07:48.768
Standard OCR trained on printed text fails on handwriting.

155
00:07:48.968 --> 00:07:51.270
Due to varied styles, cursives,

156
00:07:51.370 --> 00:07:54.774
inconsistent character formation, all break the model.

157
00:07:54.974 --> 00:07:57.977
Intelligent Character Recognition or ICR.

158
00:07:58.211 --> 00:08:01.414
is a specialized model trained on handwritten data sets

159
00:08:01.414 --> 00:08:04.717
and uses CNN+RNN architectures

160
00:08:04.717 --> 00:08:07.720
for sequence modeling and character-level predictions.

161
00:08:07.720 --> 00:08:10.423
Multilingual documents bring different challenges,

162
00:08:10.423 --> 00:08:13.025
non-standard characters, unique fonts,

163
00:08:13.025 --> 00:08:14.627
and alternate reading directions.

164
00:08:15.094 --> 00:08:17.096
right-to-left for Arabic,

165
00:08:17.196 --> 00:08:19.198
vertical text in East Asian languages.

166
00:08:19.298 --> 00:08:21.400
This requires robust multilingual models

167
00:08:21.501 --> 00:08:23.603
with automatic language detection,

168
00:08:23.903 --> 00:08:25.905
language-specific OCR engines,

169
00:08:26.105 --> 00:08:28.107
script detection and routing,

170
00:08:28.207 --> 00:08:30.610
as well as reading order adaptation per language.

171
00:08:30.977 --> 00:08:35.548
These specialized models have been the workhorses of Document AI for years.

172
00:08:35.848 --> 00:08:38.351
Each refined to excel at a specific task.

173
00:08:38.551 --> 00:08:42.755
But recently, a new paradigm has emerged, Vision-Language Models.

174
00:08:42.955 --> 00:08:47.760
These represent a fundamental shift from specialized tools to general purpose intelligence

175
00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:50.062
that can handle all these tasks and more.

176
00:08:50.363 --> 00:08:53.366
So what is a Vision-Language Model? Traditional LLMs

177
00:08:53.399 --> 00:08:55.601
purely over text tokens.

178
00:08:55.701 --> 00:08:57.904
VLMs unify vision and language,

179
00:08:58.004 --> 00:09:00.907
processing images and text simultaneously

180
00:09:01.007 --> 00:09:03.009
to form a shared semantic representation.

181
00:09:03.109 --> 00:09:06.512
This allows models to reason about what's happening in the visual scene,

182
00:09:06.612 --> 00:09:08.614
not just what words appear.

183
00:09:08.714 --> 00:09:10.917
Let's discuss what changes when we move

184
00:09:10.917 --> 00:09:13.119
from an LLM to a Vision-Language Model.

185
00:09:13.219 --> 00:09:15.821
On the left is a regular LLM.

186
00:09:15.855 --> 00:09:19.258
It takes text tokens as input, goes through the transformer,

187
00:09:19.258 --> 00:09:20.960
and you get a text output.

188
00:09:20.960 --> 00:09:23.963
Everything the model understands comes from text alone.

189
00:09:23.963 --> 00:09:26.265
On the right is a Vision-Language Model.

190
00:09:26.265 --> 00:09:28.668
The input is Image + Text.

191
00:09:29.068 --> 00:09:31.971
Before anything reaches the language model,

192
00:09:31.971 --> 00:09:35.975
the visual input has to be translated into something the LLM understands

193
00:09:35.975 --> 00:09:37.977
using a Vision Encoder.

194
00:09:37.977 --> 00:09:39.946
Then it produces the text output.

195
00:09:39.946 --> 00:09:42.448
That's what the three components below represent.

196
00:09:42.448 --> 00:09:44.450
First is the Vision Encoder.

197
00:09:44.450 --> 00:09:46.452
Models like CLIP or SigLIP

198
00:09:46.452 --> 00:09:48.654
convert pixels into visual vectors.

199
00:09:48.721 --> 00:09:50.823
Second is the Projector.

200
00:09:50.823 --> 00:09:53.726
This translation layer converts visual vectors

201
00:09:53.726 --> 00:09:56.228
into token embeddings the LLM can process.

202
00:09:56.429 --> 00:09:58.431
Third, the LLM backbone.

203
00:09:58.531 --> 00:10:02.234
At this point, a standard LLM reasons over those visual tokens

204
00:10:02.234 --> 00:10:04.337
and produces text output.

205
00:10:04.537 --> 00:10:06.238
The key takeaway is simple.

206
00:10:06.238 --> 00:10:07.940
A VLM is still an LLM

207
00:10:08.407 --> 00:10:10.509
but with a vision stack in front of it.

208
00:10:11.210 --> 00:10:14.714
can see images, but how well it understands documents

209
00:10:15.014 --> 00:10:19.318
still depends on how structure, layout, and reading order are handled.

210
00:10:19.919 --> 00:10:21.721
Vision language models are powerful,

211
00:10:21.921 --> 00:10:23.990
but they're not a complete solution on their own.

212
00:10:24.223 --> 00:10:25.992
So if you give a VLM a

213
00:10:26.192 --> 00:10:30.296
visually rich document all at once, VLMs can struggle.

214
00:10:30.596 --> 00:10:32.898
They hallucinate when visual cues are missing

215
00:10:33.032 --> 00:10:36.535
or ambiguous. They lack deterministic grounding,

216
00:10:36.535 --> 00:10:39.038
meaning they can't reliably tie their answers back

217
00:10:39.038 --> 00:10:41.340
to specific regions of the page.

218
00:10:41.540 --> 00:10:43.242
They struggle with nested layouts,

219
00:10:43.442 --> 00:10:45.845
multi-page structure and small text.

220
00:10:46.045 --> 00:10:48.447
VLMs excel when paired with structure,

221
00:10:48.447 --> 00:10:50.950
but they can't replace the structural reasoning

222
00:10:50.950 --> 00:10:53.152
required for real-world document tasks.

223
00:10:53.386 --> 00:10:58.386
One possible solution is to combine layout detection with VLM reasoning.

224
00:10:59.191 --> 00:11:03.295
The Layout Analysis we discussed earlier provides the structure foundation,

225
00:11:03.396 --> 00:11:06.899
identifying reading order, document regions, and their type.

226
00:11:07.199 --> 00:11:09.068
This structure information can then

227
00:11:09.068 --> 00:11:11.170
decide how you process each region.

228
00:11:11.470 --> 00:11:15.574
Charts and visualizations might go to a VLM with a targeted prompt.

229
00:11:15.841 --> 00:11:18.544
Tables could use either a VLM

230
00:11:18.544 --> 00:11:21.547
or a specialized Transformer depending on the complexity.

231
00:11:21.847 --> 00:11:24.250
Text regions might use traditional OCR.

232
00:11:24.717 --> 00:11:26.919
or VLM based extraction.

233
00:11:27.520 --> 00:11:31.624
In conclusion, layout detection provides the deterministic grounding,

234
00:11:31.824 --> 00:11:36.529
while the VLMs handle the elements that benefit from visual reasoning.

235
00:11:36.729 --> 00:11:40.533
One way to orchestrate this workflow is through an agentic framework.

236
00:11:40.733 --> 00:11:44.136
In the lab, you will implement a pipeline that demonstrates this.

237
00:11:44.537 --> 00:11:46.672
Starting with an input document, you

238
00:11:46.672 --> 00:11:49.141
will extract and order text using PaddleOCR.

239
00:11:49.442 --> 00:11:51.777
This gives you all the OCR text

240
00:11:51.777 --> 00:11:54.146
along with Bounding Boxes and Confidence Scores.

241
00:11:54.346 --> 00:11:56.048
You will then use LayoutReader

242
00:11:56.282 --> 00:11:58.284
to reorder the extracted text.

243
00:11:58.784 --> 00:12:02.855
You'll also run region detection using PaddleOCR's LayoutDetect

244
00:12:03.055 --> 00:12:06.459
to identify tables, charts, and text blocks.

245
00:12:06.959 --> 00:12:08.861
You'll provide all the context above,

246
00:12:08.861 --> 00:12:11.063
the OCR text in the correct order.

247
00:12:11.397 --> 00:12:13.599
region IDs and chunk types

248
00:12:13.599 --> 00:12:15.601
as context for a LangChain Agent.

249
00:12:15.601 --> 00:12:19.205
The agent will have access to two specialized tools.

250
00:12:19.205 --> 00:12:23.209
Analyze chart to send cropped chart images to a VLM,

251
00:12:23.209 --> 00:12:27.213
to extract chart type, axes, data points, and trends.

252
00:12:27.213 --> 00:12:29.915
Analyze table does the same for tables.

253
00:12:30.316 --> 00:12:33.986
extracting headers, rows, values, and notes.

254
00:12:34.487 --> 00:12:36.088
Based on the user's question,

255
00:12:36.322 --> 00:12:39.892
the agent can decide which region needs VLM analysis

256
00:12:40.192 --> 00:12:43.129
and which tools to invoke. So, let's get to it.

257
00:12:43.295 --> 00:12:45.064
By the end of the lab, you'll

258
00:12:45.064 --> 00:12:46.832
see exactly how these pieces work together

